! MULTIPLY (*), the VFL integer form, opcode abc011001.  Reference
! Manual printed p.68 defines the binary operation in two sentences:
!
!   "If binary is specified, the product of the addressed operand and
!   the accumulator field specified by the offset is placed into the
!   CLEARED ACCUMULATOR AT OFFSET 20.  Neither operand may exceed 48
!   SIGNIFICANT NUMERIC BITS.  If decimal is specified, this operation
!   has the same action as LOAD TRANSIT AND SET, except that the two
!   high-order bits of the left zeros counter are set to zeros."
!
! and Partial Field on the same page says what happens when the 48 is
! exceeded: "turned on in a binary operation if either operand exceeds
! 48 significant bits in length.  In such a case, only the 48 LOW-ORDER
! bits of the oversized operand are used in the operation.  If the
! accumulator operand was oversized, the higher-order bits are lost."
!
! OFFSET 20 IS A CONSTANT AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THIS DECK.
! The instruction's offset selects the ACCUMULATOR OPERAND, exactly as
! it does in ADD; the product is then deposited at a FIXED position,
! 20, wherever the operand came from.  Reading "at offset 20" as "at
! the offset, 20 being this example's value" is the natural misreading
! and it is wrong, so T6 and T7 run THE SAME MULTIPLICATION FROM TWO
! DIFFERENT OFFSETS and require the same answer in the same place.
!
! TWO INDEPENDENT SOURCES STATE THE RULE.  The Reference Manual states
! it normatively, above.  C22-6578 "Programming Examples", April 1961,
! states it as a working programmer's note under Problem 2.10 -- "(b)
! After a binary VFL multiply the answer is placed in the CLEARED
! ACCUMULATOR WITH OFFSET 20" -- and its Problem 2.12 carries it in a
! comment on the instruction itself, "*I(BU, 18), N, 128-18   'answer
! is at 20 offset".  Two documents, two revisions, five years apart.
!
! THE CONSTANTS ARE IBM'S.  Problem 2.12, matrix transposition, ends
!
!     M       SYN, 20      'no. of rows
!     N       SYN, 5       'no. of columns
!
! and opens "LI(BU, 18), M" / "*I(BU, 18), N", so 20 times 5 is the
! first product IBM's own example computes.  T1 and T2 are that
! product, by the storage form and by the immediate form.
!
! 20 + 96 = 116 AND THE ACCUMULATOR IS 128 BITS, which is why no
! product can overflow and why CONVERT DOUBLE reads the 20/96 field:
! printed p.70 gives DOUBLE CONVERT "an implied offset of 20 and its
! length is 96 numeric bits", and printed p.71 says "DCV is used
! instead of CV because of the offset of 20".  The constant is pinned
! three ways in the manual before this deck tests it once.
!
! WHAT EACH CHECK ESTABLISHES
!   T1    IBM's 20 times 5, the storage form.
!   T2    the same by the IMMEDIATE form, which takes its operand
!         from the address field instead of memory.
!   T3    the PARTIAL FIELD indicator is OFF after the clean
!         multiplies -- the control for T9, without which T9 proves
!         nothing about what set the bit.
!   T6    24 bits times 24 bits at offset 0: 0xFFFFFF squared is
!         0xFFFFFE000001, 48 bits, read back from offset 20.
!   T7    THE SAME MULTIPLICATION AT OFFSET 40, read back from the
!         SAME offset 20.  A model that deposited the product at the
!         instruction's offset would leave T7 holding the product
!         shifted twenty bits left of where it is read, 0x...000000,
!         and T6 and T7 would disagree.  They must be identical.
!   T8    PF TRUNCATION IS OF THE LOW 48 BITS.  The accumulator holds
!         2**48 + 3, forty-nine significant bits; the manual discards
!         the HIGH bits, so the operand is 3 and the product is 15.
!         A model that truncated the other way would compute 2**48
!         times 5, and one that refused the oversized operand would
!         leave T8 at zero.
!   T9    and PF is ON for that one, which with T3 at zero makes the
!         multiply the thing that set it.
!   T11   A STORAGE OPERAND THAT CROSSES A WORD BOUNDARY, which is
!         the shape MCP's own multiply has and the shape nothing else
!         in this deck exercises: every other operand here is a
!         word-aligned full word or an immediate, so the arm's
!         two-word path -- fieldCrosses, the second prefetch slot,
!         the (w0+1) bail-out -- would be copied code that has never
!         run.  The operand is twenty-four bits at CROSS+.56: eight
!         in CROSS's last byte and sixteen in the word after, 0xABCDEF
!         read across the seam, times twenty.  A model that took the
!         two prefetch slots in the wrong order would read 0xCDEF00
!         or 0x00ABCD here and pass every other check in this file.
!         This is the same class of hole as the crossing-field
!         connective bug of 2026-10-25 (docs/NEXT.md).
!   T10   "the CLEARED accumulator" means cleared.  Twenty bits of
!         ones are placed at offset 0, BELOW the operand, where the
!         operand shift discards them and the product at offset 20
!         cannot reach them.  They must be gone.  A model that wrote
!         only the product's own 96-bit field, or that OR'd it in,
!         would leave 0xFFFFF standing here.
!
! EVERY CONSTANT BELOW IS A FULL WORD, 64 bits with an 8-bit byte
! size, and not the 18 and 24 bits Problem 2.12 writes.  CONSECUTIVE
! DDs PACK CONTIGUOUSLY: written as "DD(BU,18), 20" and "DD(BU,18), 5"
! the two constants share a word, M occupying bits 0-17 and N bits
! 18-35, and the first assembly of this deck duly produced
! "word[1056] = 000500005fffffff" -- four constants in one word with
! the fifth straddling into the next.  The operand WIDTHS are not what
! this deck is testing, and 20 and 5 and 0xFFFFFF are the same numbers
! in an 18-bit field as in a 64-bit one, so each gets its own word and
! the addresses stay checkable.
!
! FOUR INJECTIONS, MEASURED.  They cover eight of the nine checks;
! T3 is the control for T9 and no injection should move it:
!
!   "mag: prod << 20" changed to "mag: prod << offSh", i.e. the
!     product deposited at the INSTRUCTION'S offset instead of the
!     constant -- five checks fail and T6 and T7 separate for the
!     first time: T1 = 0, T2 = 0, T6 = 0xFFFFFE0 (the product read
!     twenty bits above where it was put), T7 = 0xFFFFE00000100000
!     (read twenty bits BELOW), T8 = 0.
!   "accOp[47:0]" changed to "accOp[95:48]", i.e. the oversized
!     operand truncated to its HIGH 48 bits instead of its low --
!     T1 = T2 = T6 = T7 = 0, because those operands have nothing
!     above bit 48, and T8 = 5, because 2**48 + 3 keeps the 1 and
!     loses the 3.  The manual says the low bits survive.
!   the accumulator OR'd instead of cleared, with the PF set
!     removed (disjoint checks, so run together): T6 =
!     0xFFFFFE00000F and T7 = 0xFFFFFFF00001 (the old operand still
!     there under the product), T8 = 0x1000000F, T9 = 0, T10 =
!     0xFFFFF.
!   the two prefetch slots exchanged in the crossing-field read:
!     T11 = 0 AND NOTHING ELSE MOVES.  That single line is the
!     reason T11 exists -- the other eight checks use word-aligned
!     or immediate operands and cannot see the crossing path at all.

! A VFL FIELD IS ADDRESSED BY ITS LEFTMOST BIT, which is why T10's
! twenty-bit store is written "T10+.44" and not "T10": a twenty-bit
! field at a word's own address is that word's TOP twenty bits, and
! decks/plus_mg.strap learned this the expensive way.
!
! THE OFFSET COUNTS FROM THE ACCUMULATOR'S LOW-ORDER END, so "offset
! 0" is the bottom of the accumulator and the ONES constant added
! there sits BELOW the product's low bit at offset 20.

        SLC, (8)2000.0

! -- Problem 2.12's own first product, both forms ----------------
START   L(BU,64), M, 0
        *(BU,64), N, 0
        ST(BU,64), T1, 20          ! 20 * 5 = 100

        L(BU,64), M, 0
        *I(BU,18), 5
        ST(BU,64), T2, 20          ! 100 again, immediate

! -- the control for the PF check below --------------------------
        L(BU,1), $IND+.23, 0
        ST(BU,64), T3, 0           ! PF must still be 0 here

! -- offset 20 is a constant: same product, two offsets ----------
        L(BU,64), A, 0
        *(BU,64), B, 0
        ST(BU,64), T6, 20          ! 0xFFFFFF squared

        L(BU,64), A, 40
        *(BU,64), B, 40
        ST(BU,64), T7, 20          ! identical to T6

! -- PF, and which 48 bits survive ------------------------------
        L(BU,64), FORTY9, 0        ! 2**48 + 3: forty-nine bits
        *(BU,64), N, 0             ! operand truncates to 3
        ST(BU,64), T8, 20          ! 3 * 5 = 15
        L(BU,1), $IND+.23, 0
        ST(BU,64), T9, 0           ! PF now 1

! -- "the CLEARED accumulator" ----------------------------------
        L(BU,64), A, 40            ! operand A at offset 40
        +(BU,64), ONES, 0          ! and junk below it, at offset 0
        *(BU,64), B, 40            ! the junk shifts out of the operand
        ST(BU,20), T10+.44, 0      ! and must be cleared from the result

! -- a storage operand that STRADDLES a word boundary -----------
        L(BU,64), M, 0             ! acc := 20
        *(BU,24), CROSS+.56, 0     ! eight bits in one word, sixteen
        ST(BU,64), T11, 20         ! in the next: 0xABCDEF times 20

PASS    BEW, $

! Data, word-aligned: BEW is a half word, so without this every 64-bit
! field below would straddle two words and be refused.
        SLC, (8)2040.0

M       DD(BU,64,8), 20            ! Problem 2.12: no. of rows
N       DD(BU,64,8), 5             ! Problem 2.12: no. of columns
A       DD(BU,64,8), (16)FFFFFF
B       DD(BU,64,8), (16)FFFFFF
FORTY9  DD(BU,64,8), (16)1000000000003
ONES    DD(BU,64,8), (16)FFFFF     ! twenty ones, offset 0 to offset 19
CROSS   DD(BU,64,8), (16)AB         ! 0xAB in the LAST byte of this word
CROSS2  DD(BU,64,8), (16)CDEF000000000000

T1      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T2      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T3      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T6      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T7      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T8      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T9      DD(BU,64,8), 0
T10     DD(BU,64,8), 0
T11     DD(BU,64,8), 0

        END, START
